#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147227 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0d4530-bf50-526c-a975-941ee1fed9a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation Using the Derivative Constraint"}] :question/id #uuid "76b310bf-b592-541c-82e8-93d72bc688f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21778" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = x\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\sin(4x)$ and $y_2(x) = \\cos(4x).$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](images/28/284b6c87b050df7b5663c21dcf357b1f2fa66a86f6c28604ab6681267394ada1.png)\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n![](images/f6/f6f630f08ab3b335d43d12fa90c45df8fad0c73b83df793f43958e1ada7d199c.png)\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n![](images/32/32e3388da577fa240b722e46a266b47356239cc79821d7479ed4ea43bd4875d4.png)\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating $u_1'$ and $u_2'{:}$\n\n![](images/28/2876b8d3c2d6920da03a828ab411bb489bda9743cc0a210486c725348e14f13c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = \\sin(4x), \\qquad y_2 = \\cos(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1\\sin(4x)+u_2\\cos(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1(\\sin(4x))'+u_2 (\\cos(4x))'.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ by differentiating $y_p$ and equating this expression to our constraint on $y_p'.$\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))}^{′}+{({u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for $y_p',$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & ={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\cancel{4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\cancel{4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)} & =\\cancel{4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}-\\cancel{4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\nu_1' \\sin(4x) + u_2' \\cos(4x) = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147228 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e92641d-b0bf-579b-960b-d6024d4b99b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation by Substituting the Ansatz into the Original ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "8587baf6-8f11-56eb-8afa-dd385c0aeaeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21779" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' - 6y = p(x),\n$$\n\nwhose corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x)=e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](images/2e/2e180f0d1ea06cd71caafeafb99628fdc8560253293ee5e9375dc84d5b735a64.png)\n\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n![](images/32/32e3388da577fa240b722e46a266b47356239cc79821d7479ed4ea43bd4875d4.png)\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between $u_1', u_2',$ and $p(x){:}$\n\n![](images/28/2876b8d3c2d6920da03a828ab411bb489bda9743cc0a210486c725348e14f13c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2 = e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1e^{x}+u_2e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' =u_1(e^{x})'+u_2 (e^{-6x})'.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y & =p(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+5{y}_{p}^{′}-6{y}_{p} & =p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for $y_p$ and $y_p',$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-6x})^{′})}^{′}+5({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-6x})^{′})-6({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}) & =p(x) \\\\ {({u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x})}^{′}+5{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-30{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}-6{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} & =p(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-6x}+36{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\,+5{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-30{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}-6{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} & =p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to $u_1$ and $u_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{({e}^{x}+5{e}^{x}-6{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(36{e}^{-6x}-30{e}^{-6x}-6{e}^{-6x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-6x}=p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\nu_1'e^{x} - 6u_2'e^{-6x} = p(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147344 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a80ccfb3-b089-5b2e-9eec-171909e2d54e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Variation of Parameters to a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "cb472181-da8e-5252-9c80-4d336e2ccf9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6615" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = 6\\cos x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 6\\cos x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2.$ We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e92641d-b0bf-579b-960b-d6024d4b99b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation by Substituting the Ansatz into the Original ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d043ece-795f-53b4-badf-a88ae45b09c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{-4x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309334} {:answer/id #uuid "c615c6de-cfc5-5588-8a80-fb95fdb9cf14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310340} {:answer/id #uuid "edfbca41-ec27-5bf3-9335-58c3015e6a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310643} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ce647f-df35-5a3d-8385-26a54646b597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edfbca41-ec27-5bf3-9335-58c3015e6a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310643} :answer-field/id #uuid "3670999d-18d3-5e05-9986-335d33bba2d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "446b5de7-c479-5c00-9249-a45c5c6ab509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309270} {:answer/id #uuid "5d18cede-a1a0-5a62-b12c-a0db3805010c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{-4x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309447} {:answer/id #uuid "888891a0-b6f4-544c-bca0-954560376aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309818} {:answer/id #uuid "a011527d-7620-52d3-94fa-099b31106e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a011527d-7620-52d3-94fa-099b31106e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310042} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ab60ed-2158-5dac-8ea5-c01ac709c4f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311459} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14cbae37-20ec-54be-9725-035f89edbf1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308903} {:answer/id #uuid "80a665ec-ad09-50ef-85a7-d80890ebb11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309760} {:answer/id #uuid "d45198a2-1a16-592b-ae64-397ae6d8c000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}={g}^{′}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310461} {:answer/id #uuid "fd29c1e0-af6a-5cfd-8bc7-a45d3afb1444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}={g}^{′}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80a665ec-ad09-50ef-85a7-d80890ebb11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309760} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc582100-2b93-521b-b5c9-315b346c14f9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82772dd4-65b4-593f-86b1-6fab92304fdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-337737" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=g(x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}:$According to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between ${u}_{1}^{′},{u}_{2}^{′},$ and $g(x):$\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y & =g(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+7{y}_{p}^{′}+12{y}_{p} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′})}^{′}+7({u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′})+12({u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}) & =g(x) \\\\ {(-3{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x})}^{′}-21{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-28{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}+12{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+12{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x} & =g(x) \\\\ -3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}-21{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-28{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}+12{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+12{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{(9{e}^{-3x}-21{e}^{-3x}+12{e}^{-3x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(16{e}^{-4x}-28{e}^{-4x}+12{e}^{-4x})}_{⏟}}_{0}-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\n-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e92641d-b0bf-579b-960b-d6024d4b99b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation by Substituting the Ansatz into the Original ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4792a4b-2e9a-5f29-bbb4-2f8c8f8e0323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}={g}^{′}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374933} {:answer/id #uuid "dc08249d-d196-55f5-98d4-e65ff35ddd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374934} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2eb33e-f29a-53ad-81d9-774060a57c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}={g}^{′}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374935} {:answer/id #uuid "ef701104-0e38-51f8-bd19-76fc5e9409b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc08249d-d196-55f5-98d4-e65ff35ddd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374934} :answer-field/id #uuid "2daf61ca-2864-5046-9a57-41a4be211613" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbf02e4e-5e71-5569-923d-cf704b948e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374938} {:answer/id #uuid "124f89fa-5f05-54fe-8d9c-801391524917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374939} {:answer/id #uuid "94dedf04-0af3-5ebe-9896-ad9c7aed08cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374940} {:answer/id #uuid "aa087f5f-60f4-55f9-904c-44d0ec5f3753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{-3x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "124f89fa-5f05-54fe-8d9c-801391524917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374939} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb40b144-4943-51a4-bfb8-4f0136ae2fde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f4ed9f7-83d2-57e4-9946-9f08c5ba8a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374943} {:answer/id #uuid "80649e7c-ea15-5bb0-b71b-2016f608b537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374944} {:answer/id #uuid "ab306f37-0281-5596-857b-4e0d1ad46360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374945} {:answer/id #uuid "5883d48f-dcc8-5819-85c8-313c3cbeb50c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{-3x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab306f37-0281-5596-857b-4e0d1ad46360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374945} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff27260b-9fcc-5168-8c3c-fbea49824fda" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f89bf15e-170f-57b4-987e-95e2136f7222" :question/math-academy-id "q-337751" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-3y=g(x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}:$According to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between ${u}_{1}^{′},{u}_{2}^{′},$ and $g(x):$\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-3y & =g(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+2{y}_{p}^{′}-3{y}_{p} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′})}^{′}+2({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′})-3({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}) & =g(x) \\\\ {({u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} & =g(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{({e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(9{e}^{-3x}-6{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-3x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0d4530-bf50-526c-a975-941ee1fed9a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation Using the Derivative Constraint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb66feb1-842a-531b-951e-b8b34c2d545f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374949} {:answer/id #uuid "552e562e-794c-57c0-acfd-0da57f0138fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}=0" :db/id 